{
  "abstract": "We study private six-cliques whose coloring choices are coupled through two adjacent shared roots over a fixed 34-vertex biplanar source. Each module implements the disjunction that at least one of two prescribed terminal equalities fails. The source can satisfy all these clauses while leaving no colors for the roots, so separate module extension does not settle composition. We prove two source-family nine-coloring theorems. First, arbitrary collections of these clauses extend whenever both roots have at most eight source neighbors. An integer weighted bank handles twenty unconditioned clauses; an exhaustive conditional argument reduces the degree-eight case to eighteen exceptional neighborhoods and two further positive weight certificates. Second, for one specified ordered terminal quadruple, all biplanar extensions admit nine colors with arbitrary root-source contacts, including mixtures and repetitions of two module types. This proof replaces sampled source colorings by a complete rooted relation: eleven retained vertices determine five independent clique-matching problems. Independent enumeration and Hall checks verify the relation; literal geometric constraints and checked DRAT refutations exclude every obstructing contact pattern. A simple anchor-color preservation lemma explains why some root recolorings preserve all module clauses simultaneously. The compact supplement contains the exact inputs, independent checkers and proofs. The results close the stated composition families, not arbitrary source graphs or several distinct clauses at unrestricted root degrees, and do not change the global Earth–Moon bounds.",
